On Mon, Oct 6, 2008 at 7:08 PM, Yoav Nir <yoav.nir@gmail.com> wrote:

>
>
> On Mon, Oct 6, 2008 at 11:35 PM, H. Felton <fagricipni@gmail.com> wrote:
>
>> > H. Felton wrote:
>> >> So, "sisku" _can_ mean "ko sisku"?
>> >
>> > In general (if I understand correctly), any part of Lojban speech can be
>> > omitted when grammatically unambiguous.  In this case, the context
>> > (application command) makes it fairly clear who is to perform the
>> search.
>>
>> If it were not a command; eg, "do sisku", "lo nixli cu sisku"; I'd not
>> have been uncertain, the conversion to a command that "ko" does is
>> something that I would not have thought could be elided.
>>
>
> I think you're right, but if doesn't matter to the present question. "File"
> does not mean anything either by itself, and neither does "Font".
>
> For UI we give up grammatical purity in all languages in favor of brevity.
> No reason why lojban should be different.
>

{sisku} expands to {zo'e cu sisku zo'e zo'e vau}. Generally speaking, a
bridi with an elided x1 is interpreted as an observative, but it doesn't
have to be. So yeah {ko} can be elided, & I think it's appropriate in the UI
context (& perhaps not many others). mu'o mi'e komfo,amonan

